  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\n993 mb surface low is now located over the western end of Lake\nAmerica. Aloft, a large closed off upper level low is located\nover western NY and western PA. Our area remains within the\nmoist cyclonic flow around this storm system. Although the\nstorm's occluded front has passed through the area, widespread\nclouds remain in place and upstream satellite imagery suggests\nit will remain overcast through the rest of the day and into\ntonight, as the large storm system is expected to only slowly\ndrift northeast towards northern New England for tonight, before\npassing through Atlantic Canada for Saturday.\n\nRegional radar imagery continue to show bands of light rain\nshowers rotating around the storm system. Our area has been\nseeing occasional light showers, along with patches of drizzle\nand mist, through the day so far. Based on the latest model \nguidance, there will be a more widespread area of showers and\nlight rain passing through the area this evening, as the storm\nsystem slides by to the north. Despite the upper level passing\noverhead, the boundary layer will be warm enough for nearly all\nof this precip to be rain, and the light intensity of the\nrainfall will prevent much dynamic cooling from occurring. The\nhighest peaks of the central Adirondacks could see some wet\nsnowflakes, but no accumulation is expected, with surface temps\nholding steady across the region overnight in the mid 30s to low\n40s.\n\nAs the storm departs, most areas will be fairly dry through much\nof the day on Saturday. However, it will remain fairly cloudy\nwith plenty of lingering moisture around. With another upper\nlevel disturbance passing by to the south for late in the day,\nanother round of evening showers are possible, although \ncoverage won't be as widespread as Friday. P-type looks rain for\nmost areas, with any snow limited to the highest terrain with no\naccumulation. Total rainfall amounts will be fairly light as\nwell, mainly under a tenth of an inch, so no impacts are\nanticipated.\n\nSome brief ridging is finally expected by Sunday. With some\ndrier air moving into the area, breaks in the cloud cover is\nfinally expected by later Sunday morning. Temps will be a little\nmilder than recent days thanks to the return of some sunshine,\nwith highs reaching into the low 50s for valley areas. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAnother storm system is expected to impact the region for Sunday\nnight into Monday. This will be a fast moving storm system\nriding in the west-southwest flow aloft. With the milder flow in\nplace, temps (both aloft and at the surface) will be warm enough\nfor precip to be rain for most areas, with just some high\nterrain areas possibly seeing a changeover to snow on the\nbackside before ending, but even this is uncertain and would be\nlimited to only the highest peaks of the southern Adirondacks\nand southern Greens.  Based on the latest NBM, total rainfall \nlooks to range in the quarter to half inch range, although some \nlocally higher totals are possible in the upslope favored areas \nof the western Adirondacks. With the limited rainfall amounts, \nno hydro issues are anticipated at this time. Main timing for\nprecip looks to be after midnight Sunday night and before noon\non Monday.\n\nAs the storm departs, it is expected to deepen as it passes\ntowards Atlantic Canada. With a strengthening surface gradient,\ngusty west to northwest winds are expected behind the system for\nMonday, especially in the afternoon hours, as some breaks in the\nclouds occur and cooling temps aloft allow for better mixing.\nFrequent winds gusts look to be in the 25 to 40 mph range across\nmuch of the region. Latest NBM shows fairly high confidence \n(50% to 80% chance) for gusts to even exceed 40 mph in the\nMohawk Valley, Capital Region, eastern Catskills and Berkshires,\nso there could be the possibility of some downed tree limbs and\npower outages. Probabilities lower for gusts to reach or exceed\n47 mph, but there is some localized areas where the probability\ndoes reach 30%-50%, specifically in the Capital Region and\nBerkshires, so some localized gusts in the solid Wind Advisory\ncriteria are possible. Will continue to monitor the wind\npotential for Monday closely, as some wind impacts look to be a\npossibility.",
